<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Baikal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Baikal is a rift lake in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the deepest lake in the world at about 1,640 metres and the largest freshwater lake by volume, holding more than a fifth of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water. Fed chiefly by the Selenga, it is home to many species found nowhere else, including the Baikal seal. Peoples such as the Merkits and the Buryats lived near its shores.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-baikal.jpg" alt="Lake Baikal" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques Descloitres, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA/GSFC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baikal.A2001296.0420.250m-NASA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Baikal lies in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> between Irkutsk Oblast to the north-west and the Republic of Buryatia to the south-east. It sits in a rift valley formed by the Baikal Rift Zone, where the Earth's crust is slowly pulling apart. The lake is 636 km long and 79 km wide, and its maximum depth is 1,642 metres. The rift is young and active, widening about 4 mm a year; the area is seismically active. The lake has three basins, North, Central and South. It drains into the Angara and is among the clearest in the world. Its surface freezes from early January to early May or June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Baikal area has a long history of human habitation. At Mal'ta, some 160 km north-west of the lake, the remains of a young male known as Mal'ta Boy point to the Mal'ta–Buret' culture about 24,000 years ago. The Kurykans gave the lake a name that translates as much water; the Buryats later called it Baygal nuur, the natural lake. It lay in the former northern territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> confederation and is one site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xiongnu-war">Han–Xiongnu War</a>, and Han records called it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, one of the semi-mythical Four Seas. It was under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>'s Anbei Protectorate from 647 to 682.</p><p>Little was known to Europeans about the lake until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> expanded into the area in the 1600s. The Baykalsk Pulp and Paper Mill, built in 1966 directly on the shore, discharged waste into the lake; Soviet scientists protested against it, and it closed in November 2008.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world, estimated at 25 to 30 million years old, and holds more than 1,000 plant species and 2,500 animal species, many of them found nowhere else, among them the Baikal seal, or nerpa. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1996. Buryat peoples live on its eastern side, where they raise goats, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and horses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Baikal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Mongolia it was absorbed into Mongolian Buddhism, surviving unabsorbed only in the far north, around Lake Khövsgöl and <b>Lake Baikal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Merkits lived along the lower Selenga river south of <b>Lake Baikal</b>, on the edge of the Siberian forest.</blockquote><a href="/state/merkit-confederation">Merkit confederation</a></li></ul></section>
